message: "If you use this work or its experimental quantum hardware data, please cite it as below."
title: "triangle-anchor-memory: Nine-Phase Quantum Hardware Experiment Series and Noise Audit on IBM Heron"
version: "3.1.1"
date-released: "2026-11-18"
repository-code: "https://github.com/silentnoisehun/triangle-anchor-memory"
license: MIT
authors:
  - name: "Róbert, Máté"
    family-names: "Róbert"
    given-names: "Máté"
    affiliation: "Silent Coder Labs"
    orcid: "0009-0003-3986-6039"
abstract: >-
  Nine-phase preregistered experiment series (1011 circuits) on IBM Heron r2
  processors testing driven loop topologies (ring, star, wheel, multi-cell networks)
  for excitation retention. Phase 1 (threshold gate) ran on ibm_kingston; the
  other phases ran on ibm_marrakesh, although some plans named ibm_kingston.

  Result: consistent with textbook gate noise; no memory effect is demonstrated.
  The wheel's 37.11% central population (Phase 4, k = 32) matches a noisy Aer
  prediction of 37.99%, computed from an ibm_kingston calibration snapshot, and
  only at k = 32 (at k = 4 and k = 8 the deviation is about +31 and −31 percentage
  points). The static, non-rotating control gave 45.51%, higher than the rotating
  drive. The earlier Z = 13.63 "anomaly" is withdrawn: it came from a simulation
  baseline without idle T1 relaxation. In the multi-qubit phases, circuits
  prepared with one excitation show many multi-excitation shots (e.g. Phase 8,
  k = 12: 27.7% of shots have two or more excited satellites, which is why the
  satellite marginals sum to 104.5%). The apparent 72.1% collective retention
  mostly counts incoherent excitations from gate errors. Transverse coherence
  decays to about 0.07 at k = 32.

  Erratum 2026-11-18: corrected audit phase labels, the multi-excitation census,
  Phase 7 P(S3) (28.5%, not 30.1%) and backend provenance. Raw counts, job IDs
  and frozen preregistrations are unchanged.
keywords:
  - "superconducting qubits"
  - "IBM Heron"
  - "noise audit"
  - "excitation retention"
  - "preregistered experiment"
  - "negative result"
  - "reproducibility"
